<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # In Ten Years, I Will... – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Future Plans and Time Capsule</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> In Ten Years, I Will...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Facilitate discussions on project goals and tasks. Text: Clarify written instructions in collaborative projects.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can facilitate a discussion on future goals for the time capsule using gestures (e.g., "What should we put in our time capsule?" while showing pictures of items like photos and letters.). Can clarify project instructions by listing steps on the board (e.g., "Step 1: Choose items; Step 2: Write descriptions."). Can help classmates understand project instructions by rephrasing in simple terms or using the native language when needed (e.g., "Everyone must bring a personal object for the project," while pointing to the list of items).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of various personal objects (e.g., old photos, letters, small toys, newspaper clippings). Students are asked to briefly discuss in pairs what these items might represent if put in a "time capsule" for the future. The teacher prompts: "Imagine you found a time capsule from 10 years ago. What kinds of objects would you expect to find inside? What stories might they tell about someone's `goals` or `aspirations`?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to facilitate a mini-discussion by asking a student about a chosen item and rephrasing their response for clarity. E.g., Teacher: "Carla, what `opportunities` does this photo of a graduation cap represent for you?" Carla: "It shows I want to finish `education`." Teacher: "So, Carla believes this item `symbolizes` her `goal` to `achieve` a higher `education` in the future." The teacher also models the pronunciation of /iː/ in "achieve" and "believe."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you `mediate` a discussion, are you telling people what to think, or helping them understand each other?" (Expected answer: Helping them understand.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I rephrase what Carla said, am I changing her meaning or just saying it in a clearer way?" (Expected answer: Clearer way.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a "Project Brief: Time Capsule Video" handout and a short video clip (1-2 min) of an older student explaining their own time capsule items and future plans, using modals for possibilities and future continuous. The handout outlines the 21st Century Skills Project, providing clear, step-by-step instructions for creating the video. It also includes an example of a student's script using target grammar and vocabulary (e.g., "I `might pursue` a `career` in `engineering`, and in ten years, I `will be working` as a `software developer`.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student is able to understand and interpret instructions for a collaborative project related to future aspirations and communicate their understanding. (Sociolinguistic: Organize class discussions about personal goals for the future, assigning roles to peers).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What is the main `goal` of the Time Capsule Video project?" (Teacher encourages students to summarize in their own words.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match key project terms (e.g., "submission deadline," "required items," "video length") from the handout to their definitions or specific requirements listed in a scrambled order.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "The project requires you to `volunteer` for `community service` before recording your video." (False, focuses on future plans)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Accuracy focus on Mediation</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Mediation of instructions and discussions, using modals for career `possibilities`, future continuous for ongoing `plans`, reported speech for `advice`. Target vocabulary: `career`, `future`, `goals`, `possibilities`, `advice`, `education`, `training`, `technology`, `engineering`, `medicine`, `software developer`, `data analyst`, `internship`, `experience`, `skills`, `leadership`, `problem-solving`, `critical thinking`, `community service`, `opportunities`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Clarifying Instructions:</b> In small groups (3-4 students), the teacher provides a slightly confusing or incomplete project instruction from the handout (e.g., "Include objects that show `future` `goals`."). Students must `clarify` the instruction by rephrasing it in simpler terms or listing specific examples. The teacher emphasizes using phrases like "That means...", "In other words...", or "For example..." and highlights words with /iː/ like `believe`.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Mediating Advice:</b> Students receive short scenarios about classmates struggling with project ideas (e.g., "Juan is unsure what `career` to `pursue`."). In pairs, one student acts as the "mediator," providing `advice` using reported speech based on `suggestions` from a hypothetical `mentor` (e.g., "`My teacher said that he should consider` an `internship` to gain `experience`."). The other student `explains` how that `advice` could `help` Juan with his `future` `plans`.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Facilitating Discussion:</b> Students take turns `facilitating` a brief discussion with their partner about their chosen item for the time capsule. One student presents an item and their `future` `goal` related to it (e.g., "I am including a photo of my robotics project because it represents my `goal` to become an electronic `engineer` in the future."). The partner then asks clarifying questions (e.g., "So, you `believe` that photo shows your `skills` in `technology`? What `opportunities` might that `career` provide?"), ensuring clear communication and using stress in words like "`problem-solving`" and "`leadership`."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Communicative task where students USE the language</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Group Discussion and Project Plan Explanation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students form groups of 4-5. Their task is to `facilitate` a group discussion to outline the steps for their "Time Capsule Video" project and to `clarify` any remaining doubts for their peers. Each group will assign roles: one "Facilitator" (responsible for `organizing` the discussion and ensuring everyone participates), one "Clarifier" (responsible for rephrasing instructions or `explaining` `vocabulary` if needed, even using simple L1 if absolutely essential for understanding, as per the learning outcome), and one "Summarizer" (responsible for synthesizing the group's decisions). The Facilitator starts by asking, "What should we all `achieve` for our time capsule videos this week?" The Clarifier ensures everyone understands the project requirements and explains `complex` concepts like "`critical thinking`" or "`high-demand careers`" in simpler terms. Students discuss their chosen `future` `goals` and how they `will be working` towards them, incorporating specific `advice` (`e.g. "My classmate `suggested` that I should include a drawing of my future office."`). The Summarizer reports the group's `plan` to the class, using modals of `possibility` and `future continuous`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Actions:</b> Circulate, observe group dynamics, provide support and correct language errors unobtrusively. Offer `suggestions` on how to `mediate` more effectively, e.g., "Can you rephrase that instruction for John? He still seems a bit confused." or "What `advice` did the handout `recommend` for choosing items?"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group presents their summarized "Time Capsule Video Project Plan" (from Stage 4) to the class. The "Summarizer" from each group outlines the agreed-upon `goals`, `tasks`, and any `clarified` instructions. Other students use a simple peer assessment form to evaluate the `clarity` and `cohesion` of the presentation, focusing on how well instructions were `mediated` and `explained`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist & Peer Assessment):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Mediation:</b> Did the "Clarifier" effectively rephrase instructions and `explain` `vocabulary` when needed? (1 = Not clear, 3 = Mostly clear, 5 = Very clear)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Facilitation of Discussion:</b> Did the "Facilitator" successfully `organize` the discussion and encourage participation? (1-5 scale)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Language:</b> Did the group use modals (e.g., `might pursue`, `will be working`) and relevant `future-oriented` `vocabulary` (e.g., `career`, `goals`, `opportunities`, `leadership`)? (Yes/No with examples)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Intelligible pronunciation of /iː/ words (e.g., `achieve`, `engineering`) and stress in multisyllabic words (`critical thinking`). (Observed / Not observed)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Did all group members contribute to the `mediation` process? (Yes/No)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one specific instruction you helped a classmate `understand` today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did using `reported speech` or `future continuous` help you `explain` `advice` or `plans`?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well do you feel you can now `facilitate` a discussion or `clarify` project instructions for your peers? What was the most challenging part of `mediating` today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"In what `future` situations might your `mediation skills` be `relevant` or useful, outside of English class?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What `mediation` strategies worked best for students to `clarify` project instructions and `facilitate` discussions? Were students able to use the target grammar (modals for `possibilities`, `future continuous`, `reported speech`) and `vocabulary` effectively during their group work and presentations? What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with `mediating` `complex` ideas or `explaining` in English? (e.g., providing more sentence frames for `clarification`, more practice with `reported speech`.)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This is Lesson 5, the final lesson of the unit focusing on the 21st Century Skills Project (Time Capsule Video). The next lesson will begin a new theme and sequence of skills, likely starting with `Listening` and `Vocabulary Introduction` related to a new topic.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>